WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.519 --> 00:00:17.863
Two hours later Brody had moved on from practicing for the hoplitodromos race to practicing for the wrestling

2
00:00:17.923 --> 00:00:18.503
event again.

3
00:00:18.523 --> 00:00:22.066
Brody hadn't wrestled much, but he wasn't worried.

4
00:00:23.007 --> 00:00:28.851
Today he was practicing against Demeter's champion, a tall, thin youth with a big nose.

5
00:00:28.871 --> 00:00:30.532
"Got you!"

6
00:00:30.952 --> 00:00:34.535
Brody cried as he pinned the other athlete for the third time in a row.

7
00:00:35.957 --> 00:00:37.858
Demeter's champion gritted his teeth.

8
00:00:37.878 --> 00:00:40.441
"Never mind," he said.

9
00:00:40.461 --> 00:00:45.725
"I may not be the best at wrestling.But I'll beat you at boxing every time!"

10
00:00:46.987 --> 00:00:48.728
Brody let him go and stood up.

11
00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:51.411
"Boxing?" he said to Hermes.

12
00:00:51.431 --> 00:00:52.832
"Do I have to do that?"

13
00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:55.614
"Of course," Hermes said.

14
00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:57.296
"We'll practice that tomorrow.

15
00:00:58.388 --> 00:01:01.289
I've already started setting up some sparring partners for you."

16
00:01:02.669 --> 00:01:07.531
Before Brody could say anything else, a loud buzzing sound filled the gymnasium.

17
00:01:07.551 --> 00:01:09.371
"What's that?"

18
00:01:09.931 --> 00:01:10.972
Julia exclaimed.

19
00:01:12.092 --> 00:01:13.312
Markos covered his head.

20
00:01:13.332 --> 00:01:14.633
"Look out above!"

21
00:01:15.673 --> 00:01:21.114
Brody glanced up just in time to see a huge black cloud descending on the gymnasium.

22
00:01:22.275 --> 00:01:26.936
When it got closer, he saw that it was actually a swarm of large insects.

23
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.703
"Gross!" Brody covered his head as the first insects hit him.

24
00:01:32.723 --> 00:01:34.864
"What are those things?"

25
00:01:34.884 --> 00:01:38.887
"Cicadas." Hermes sounded disgusted.

26
00:01:38.907 --> 00:01:41.689
"Apollo! Did you have something to do with this?"

27
00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:49.734
Apollo was standing nearby, grinning as the other gods' champions raced for cover from the rain of singing

28
00:01:49.795 --> 00:01:57.600
insects. Meanwhile Apollo's own champion continued to jog around the track, untouched by the cicadas.

29
00:01:58.716 --> 00:02:01.438
"What makes you think I had anything to do with this?"

30
00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:03.900
Apollo asked, still grinning.

31
00:02:04.981 --> 00:02:08.143
Cicadas were pelting every inch of Brody's head and body.

32
00:02:09.344 --> 00:02:10.765
One even crawled into his mouth.

33
00:02:10.785 --> 00:02:18.891
"Yuck!" he exclaimed, spitting it out and then running to join Julia and Markos, who were huddled beneath

34
00:02:18.931 --> 00:02:20.212
the shelter of an overhang.

35
00:02:20.232 --> 00:02:22.834
"Enough, brother!"

36
00:02:23.334 --> 00:02:25.196
Artemis stepped forward, frowning.

37
00:02:26.524 --> 00:02:30.066
Ignoring the insects raining down on her, she lifted her bow.

38
00:02:30.086 --> 00:02:32.787
"Feathered friends, help us!"

39
00:02:35.408 --> 00:02:38.969
A loud, piercing shriek seemed to tear the sky in two.

40
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.471
A moment later something scurried into the gymnasium.

41
00:02:44.772 --> 00:02:45.432
"What's that?"

42
00:02:45.452 --> 00:02:48.753
Brody asked, peering through the veil of cicadas.

43
00:02:48.773 --> 00:02:52.495
"It looks like a bird," Markos said.

44
00:02:52.515 --> 00:02:54.536
"It's eating the cicadas!"

45
00:02:55.892 --> 00:02:58.413
"It's a guinea fowl," Julia explained.

46
00:02:58.433 --> 00:03:00.513
"My neighbors used to raise them.

47
00:03:00.993 --> 00:03:02.094
They're really noisy.

48
00:03:02.514 --> 00:03:03.954
And they'll eat anything."

49
00:03:03.974 --> 00:03:07.995
"Look!" Markos pointed.

50
00:03:08.015 --> 00:03:09.616
"Here come more of them!"

51
00:03:10.496 --> 00:03:13.637
Dozens of guinea fowl were running or flying into view.

52
00:03:14.577 --> 00:03:17.018
They immediately began gobbling up the cicadas.

53
00:03:17.718 --> 00:03:20.158
Some of them let out sharp cries as they fed.

54
00:03:20.178 --> 00:03:22.599
"This is ridiculous!"

55
00:03:23.399 --> 00:03:27.873
Ares marched over to Apollo and Artemis, cicadas crunching beneath his feet.

56
00:03:27.893 --> 00:03:32.394
"How can any of us train under these conditions?"

57
00:03:33.954 --> 00:03:38.196
"Sorry." Apollo shrugged, not looking sorry at all.

58
00:03:38.216 --> 00:03:44.457
"I was just testing out a little trick I might try on the mighty Alcides."

59
00:03:46.278 --> 00:03:50.479
"Hmm." Ares suddenly looked much less angry.

60
00:03:50.499 --> 00:03:57.354
"Now that you mention it, I have a few things in mind for Zeus' mighty champion as well."

61
00:03:59.335 --> 00:04:04.218
He raised his spear to his own head just as his red hair burst into flame.

62
00:04:05.059 --> 00:04:09.161
He lit his spear, and then twirled it, shooting fire at the cicadas.

63
00:04:10.322 --> 00:04:14.064
A guinea fowl let out a squawk as a spark just missed its head.

64
00:04:14.084 --> 00:04:16.725
"Maybe we should get out of here."

65
00:04:17.225 --> 00:04:18.546
Julia sounded nervous.

66
00:04:19.847 --> 00:04:21.368
"Things are getting a little dangerous."

67
00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:23.280
Hermes sighed.

68
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:29.023
"I suppose we won't get anything done until the others have had their fun," he said.

69
00:04:29.043 --> 00:04:32.004
"Besides, it's almost dinnertime.

70
00:04:32.524 --> 00:04:32.944
Let's go."

71
00:04:35.565 --> 00:04:37.466
They headed back to Markos' house.

72
00:04:37.486 --> 00:04:42.888
"Are the other gods really going to sabotage Alcides, like they said?"

73
00:04:43.428 --> 00:04:44.749
Julia asked Hermes.

74
00:04:45.929 --> 00:04:46.870
He looked surprised.

75
00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:48.090
"Of course!

76
00:04:48.671 --> 00:04:49.891
What a silly question, girl."

77
00:04:51.012 --> 00:04:51.852
Brody frowned.

78
00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:57.163
"But the Olympics are supposed to be about who's the best athlete," he said.

79
00:04:57.183 --> 00:05:01.665
"How can you figure that out when you're all trying to cheat all the time?"

80
00:05:03.066 --> 00:05:05.968
He was afraid that Hermes might get mad at his question.

81
00:05:07.228 --> 00:05:09.229
Instead, the god laughed.

82
00:05:11.871 --> 00:05:13.612
"Oh, you mortals!

83
00:05:14.072 --> 00:05:16.333
You're always getting worked up about things like that."

84
00:05:17.354 --> 00:05:18.815
He patted Brody on the head.

85
00:05:18.835 --> 00:05:20.876
"Don't worry about it.

86
00:05:21.256 --> 00:05:23.518
It's not as if any of this matters, after all."

87
00:05:25.079 --> 00:05:25.739
"What do you mean?"

88
00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.140
Julia asked.

89
00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.462
"I thought the Olympic Games were super important.

90
00:05:31.303 --> 00:05:34.225
Don't the games help to keep peace among all the Greek cities?

91
00:05:35.026 --> 00:05:36.827
And honor Zeus and the other gods?"

92
00:05:37.767 --> 00:05:41.110
Hermes pointed to a dog chasing a rat along a path.

93
00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:45.653
"To those creatures, their game is important," he said.

94
00:05:45.673 --> 00:05:48.835
"To you mortals, the Games are important.

95
00:05:49.395 --> 00:05:49.876
To us?"

96
00:05:50.452 --> 00:05:51.312
He shrugged again.

97
00:05:51.332 --> 00:05:54.254
"The Games are amusing, yes.

98
00:05:55.494 --> 00:05:58.115
They bring us pride and a chance to brag, certainly.

99
00:05:58.696 --> 00:06:00.756
But important?"

100
00:06:01.877 --> 00:06:03.097
He laughed loudly.

101
00:06:04.278 --> 00:06:08.720
Brody was still thinking about what Hermes had said when the family finished dinner that evening.

102
00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:13.542
He waited until he, Julia, and Markos were alone in the courtyard.

103
00:06:13.562 --> 00:06:16.443
"So what did Hermes mean earlier?"

104
00:06:16.883 --> 00:06:19.736
he asked Julia and Markos.

105
00:06:19.756 --> 00:06:21.497
"Doesn't he care about the Olympics?"

106
00:06:21.517 --> 00:06:25.260
"I told you before," Markos said.

107
00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.982
"The gods are different from mortals.

108
00:06:28.542 --> 00:06:32.585
We are playthings to them—pets, like that dog we saw."

109
00:06:34.006 --> 00:06:34.907
Julia frowned.

110
00:06:34.927 --> 00:06:39.930
"I don't even think they see us the way we see dogs or other pets," she said.

111
00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:42.652
"We're more like ants to them.

112
00:06:44.233 --> 00:06:48.096
They make us race each other, and then they pull our legs off or stomp on us for

113
00:06:48.156 --> 00:07:18.897
fun!" "You're lucky you're Hermes' champion, Brody," Markos said.

114
00:07:18.917 --> 00:07:19.577
"What are you talking about?"

115
00:07:19.637 --> 00:07:19.997
Julia blurted out.

116
00:07:20.077 --> 00:07:21.238
"Hermes might not ever let us ever return home!"

117
00:07:21.258 --> 00:07:23.179
"That's true," Markos admitted with a grim expression.

118
00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:25.000
"But I hate to think of what might happen to Hera's hero if he fails to defeat Alcides.

119
00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.741
Or what could happen to Alcides himself if he disappoints Zeus."

120
00:07:26.761 --> 00:07:29.942
"Hmm Brody hadn't realized that some of the gods could be so cruel.

121
00:07:29.963 --> 00:07:33.184
"Well, it sounds as if Alcides might not even make it to the Olympics," he pointed out.

122
00:07:33.204 --> 00:07:35.305
"I mean, every god out there is going to try to take him down, right?"

123
00:07:35.325 --> 00:07:37.426
"Except Athena and Zeus," Julia said.

124
00:07:37.446 --> 00:07:38.587
"Okay, maybe you're right, Markos.

125
00:07:39.147 --> 00:07:40.328
Maybe it could be worse."
